window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["1a"]={"https://theconversation.com/nothing-to-see-here-cricket-australias-next-integrity-test-is-its-own-investigation-296395":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"81ceb890b63f926a9dc0437281e69c1b9e9a1a8a037bd8e58a3e55c80fd6b0b2","read":"https://theconversation.com/nothing-to-see-here-cricket-australias-next-integrity-test-is-its-own-investigation-296395","bytes":181080,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Nothing to see here? Cricket Australia’s next integrity test is its own investigation","by":[{"n":"James Skinner","r":"Dean Newcastle Business School/Professor of Sport Business, University of Newcastle"}],"pub":"2026-11-11T01:58:33Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Eight years after sandpaper brought ",["Australian cricket into disgrace","https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/10/sandpapergate-cricket-australia-ball-tampering-saga-usman-khawaja-book"],", the ",["latest integrity question","https://theconversation.com/did-australias-cricket-leaders-sandpaper-over-the-truth-new-ball-tampering-revelations-suggest-so-296128"]," concerns the investigation that followed."]},{"k":"p","s":["The 2018 controversy was ",["this week reignited","https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/nov/10/sandpapergate-cricket-australia-ball-tampering-saga-usman-khawaja-book"]," by retired batter Usman Khawaja, who said the ball tampering incident wasn’t a plan hatched by three batters only (Steve Smith, David Warner and Cameron Bancroft). Instead, ",["the whole team","https://www.theage.com.au/sport/cricket/people-have-different-views-cricket-australia-refuses-to-re-open-sandpaper-scandal-investigation-20261110-p61csw.html"]," was in on it."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yesterday, his long-time friend ",["Warner said the revelations","https://www.smh.com.au/sport/cricket/people-have-books-to-sell-maddinson-questions-timing-of-khawaja-revelations-20261109-p61ckk.html"]," were “100% accurate”, heaping pressure on other members of the 2018 team as well as Cricket Australia. Other players have ",["denied knowledge","https://www.nine.com.au/sport/cricket/australia-ball-tampering-sandpapergate-news-mitchell-starc-reaction-usman-khawaja-book-20261110-p61cwe.html"]," in recent days."]},{"k":"p","s":["But Cricket Australia’s inquiry after the 2018 incident, which only ",["lasted several days","https://www.cricket.com.au/news/3294494"]," and resulted in Smith, Warner and Bancroft being suspended, is firmly in the spotlight."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Did Australia’s cricket leaders sandpaper over the truth? New ball tampering revelations suggest so","https://theconversation.com/did-australias-cricket-leaders-sandpaper-over-the-truth-new-ball-tampering-revelations-suggest-so-296128"]]},{"k":"h","s":["What has been Cricket Australia’s reaction?"]},{"k":"p","s":["This week’s revelations challenge the reach of Cricket Australia’s 2018 inquiry. The governing body, though, has ",["ruled out reopening it","https://www.cricket.com.au/news/4607191/cricket-australia-will-not-reopen-sandpaper-ball-tampering-investigation-2026-todd-greenberg-usman-khawaja-david-warner"],", despite ",["calls for it to do so","https://www.cricinfo.com/story/australia-news-david-warner-throws-his-support-behind-usman-khawaja-on-sandpapergate-revelations-1561973"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Chief executive Todd Greenberg said other players disputed Khawaja’s version and the board believes there is ",["no compelling reason","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-10/cricket-australia-no-sandpapergate-investigation/107367290"]," to revisit the findings."]},{"k":"p","s":["That disagreement deserves independent assessment. It does not establish that other players participated in the Cape Town plan, or that investigators concealed evidence."]},{"k":"p","s":["But it does raise a question of how a governing body should respond when new testimony challenges the completeness of an earlier investigation."]},{"k":"h","s":["What 2018 the inquiry established"]},{"k":"p","s":["In March 2018, Cricket Australia concluded prior knowledge of the Cape Town plan was confined to Smith, Warner and Bancroft. Its public account said no other player, support staff member or coach ",["knew beforehand","https://www.cricket.com.au/news/3292164/ca-findings-burning-questions-answered"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The inquiry unfolded between Tests, with immediate decisions needed. Reporting at the time described interviews with selected players and support staff, with ",["findings within days","https://www.cricket.com.au/news/3293556"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Speed alone does not invalidate an investigation. But an inquiry designed to support urgent decisions should be judged against what it examined, what evidence it collected and how far its conclusions could reasonably extend."]},{"k":"p","s":["Khawaja says he ",["was not interviewed","https://www.nine.com.au/sport/cricket/news-usman-khawaja-book-sandpaper-details-australia-south-africa-cameron-bancroft-steve-smith-david-warner-20261107-p61c6y.html"],". He describes broader team discussions about altering the ball and alleges sandpaper had been used in the preceding Test. Warner has ",["now criticised","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-09/cricket-warner-backs-khawaja-ball-tampering-claims-slams-probe/107365778"]," the investigation."]},{"k":"p","s":["These claims require careful separation. Knowledge of earlier discussions, knowledge of earlier conduct and advance knowledge of the specific Cape Town plan are different propositions. Evidence supporting one does not automatically establish the others."]},{"k":"p","s":["The bowlers who played at Cape Town publicly denied prior knowledge of the sandpaper plan ",["in 2021","https://www.cricket.com.au/news/4607191/cricket-australia-will-not-reopen-sandpaper-ball-tampering-investigation-2026-todd-greenberg-usman-khawaja-david-warner"],". Those denials matter. So does testing new accounts against the available records, rather than treating either recollection or denial as conclusive."]},{"k":"p","s":["A review does not need to produce agreement among former teammates. Investigations weigh conflicting accounts – they do not require unanimity."]},{"k":"p","s":["The practical question is whether the evidence supports the confidence attached to the original findings."]},{"k":"h","s":["Accountability and integrity queries"]},{"k":"p","s":["The slippery slope here is a gradual weakening of accountability. An urgent finding becomes an accepted public account. Over time, defending that account can become an institutional priority, even when questions arise about its boundaries."]},{"k":"p","s":["The concern is Cricket Australia could become more focused on defending its original findings than testing their limits. However, raising this concern does not prove the governing body has resisted scrutiny to protect its reputation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Its current leadership may genuinely believe the original conclusions remain sound. But independent scrutiny would provide a stronger basis for that confidence."]},{"k":"p","s":["The governing body has already accepted responsibility extends beyond individual players. It has previously commissioned independent scrutiny of its own leadership and culture: in 2018, The Ethics Centre – an independent, Sydney-based not-for-profit organisation – examined organisational values, leadership and the conditions that ",["contributed to the scandal","https://ethics.org.au/casestudy/cricket-australia/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["That review addressed a different question. Understanding the environment in which misconduct occurred did not amount to reinvestigating who knew about the Cape Town plan. Cultural reform cannot, by itself, establish that the original factual inquiry was sufficiently comprehensive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cycling offers a relevant precedent. The ",["Cycling Independent Reform Commission","https://velo-cdn.outsideonline.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/CIRC-Report-2015.pdf"],", whose report appeared in 2015, examined doping alongside allegations concerning the international governing body’s handling and investigation of it."]},{"k":"p","s":["The scandals differ substantially. The useful comparison concerns investigative accountability, because a governing body’s response to misconduct can itself become a legitimate subject of independent examination."]},{"k":"h","s":["What should happen from here?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cricket Australia need not begin with another full investigation. It could appoint an independent reviewer to assess the original inquiry’s terms, interview coverage, surviving records and the significance of the new accounts."]},{"k":"p","s":["A review could establish whether Khawaja’s account contains material information investigators lacked, whether relevant witnesses were omitted and whether those gaps could affect the published conclusions."]},{"k":"p","s":["People whose conduct is questioned should have a fair opportunity to respond."]},{"k":"p","s":["The recommendation might be no further investigation is warranted. It might identify unresolved questions requiring targeted interviews or a broader inquiry. Independence does not predetermine the answer; it makes the route to that answer more credible."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cricket Australia should publish the review’s reasoning, with protections for confidential information, and explain its decision."]},{"k":"p","s":["Any limits created by missing records or the passage of time should be acknowledged."]},{"k":"h","s":["Is Cricket Australia committed to integrity?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Australian cricket’s credibility depends on the confidence of other cricketing nations, supporters and commercial partners."]},{"k":"p","s":["These stakeholders can reasonably ask whether Australian cricket applies comparable standards of accountability to players and administrators."]},{"k":"p","s":["Leaving the findings untested risks a perception that integrity demands stop at the governing body’s door. A reasoned, independently informed decision would demonstrate that Cricket Australia’s commitment includes scrutiny of its own processes."]}],"chars":6463},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/palau":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"976b372665ad80a31be8709d4cc58a9beddda27b5e20ae856b13ef3030353bd0","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/palau","bytes":37604,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Palau travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2012-12-20T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2025-12-10T13:25:21+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/palau/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Palau’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Palau set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Palau Consulate in Batley in the UK","https://palauconsulate.org.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive in Palau and at least 2 blank pages."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Palau for up to 30 days without a pre-arranged visa."]},{"k":"p","s":["Border officials will issue a 30-day tourist visa on arrival. You may need to show proof of onward or return travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact the Bureau of Immigration before you travel if you’re likely to seek an extension of your stay."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: (+680) 488 2498 or 2678 (Monday to Friday, 8am to 5pm)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Email: imm@palaunet.com"]},{"k":"h","s":["Border control"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must sign the ",["Palau Pledge","https://www.palaupledge.com/"]," which is stamped in your passport on arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must also complete the Palau entry form within 72 hours of your arrival."]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry by sea"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you arrive by sea, you must give at least 48 hours’ notice of your arrival so that immigration formalities can be arranged."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must complete the Palau entry form within 72 hours of your arrival."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Palau guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/172/palau#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Palau. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty on the Palau entry form."]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare personal items worth more than 400 US dollars and cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 US dollars or more."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to bring ",["reef-toxic sunscreen","https://www.palaugov.pw/the-republic-of-palau-adopts-the-worlds-strictest-national-sunscreen-standard/"]," into Palau. You’ll see disposal bins for banned sunscreens. If customs officials find reef-toxic sunscreen in your baggage, they will confiscate it. Permitted sunscreens generally have an active ingredient of zinc oxide or titanium dioxide."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Palau"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Palau, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Crime levels are low, and you’re more likely to experience theft of unattended items than confrontational crime. Take sensible precautions to protect your belongings and keep your accommodation secure."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been an increase in credit card fraud. This may include credit card skimming devices or other types of data theft. Take care when paying with credit cards or withdrawing money from ATMs."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are heavy penalties for all drug offences. If you’re convicted of possessing even a small amount of an illegal drug, you could get a fine or a prison sentence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal drinking age in Palau is 21 years. It is illegal to drink alcohol in public, except on licensed premises."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Unexploded bombs and weapons"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are unexploded World War 2 bombs and weapons in Palau. There is a higher risk on the islands of Peleliu and Angaur, where there were major battles in the Pacific campaign."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be alert to the risk if exploring caves or hiking off main routes. Do not touch anything that appears to be made of metal. Take pictures and report anything out of the ordinary to the authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming and diving safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take local advice before swimming or diving from high rocks. There are over 60 vertical drop-offs in the diving areas and some are for experienced divers only."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ensure you have comprehensive travel insurance that includes medical evacuation and covers your planned activities."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scuba diving"]},{"k":"p","s":["Diving school standards are not always as high as in the UK. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check a dive operator’s credentials"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure you’re covered by insurance"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure safety equipment is available on the boat, particularly oxygen"]},{"k":"li","s":["ask about safety precautions, including the ability to transfer divers to Palau’s only hyperbaric chamber"]},{"k":"p","s":["The hyperbaric chamber is at the Belau National Hospital in Koror, less than 2 hours from most locations."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have not had any previous diving experience:"]},{"k":"li","s":["ask your dive instructor to explain what cover they offer before signing up"]},{"k":"li","s":["check what to do if something goes wrong, including how to call for help while at sea"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Reef-toxic sunscreen"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to use ",["reef-toxic sunscreen","https://www.palaugov.pw/the-republic-of-palau-adopts-the-worlds-strictest-national-sunscreen-standard/"]," in Palau. Permitted sunscreens generally have an active ingredient of zinc oxide or titanium dioxide."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are penalties for selling reef-toxic sunscreen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Palau, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Palau for up to 30 days. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well. After 30 days you must apply for a Palauan driving licence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Main roads are in reasonable condition. Inland roads may be narrow and damaged by flooding during the summer months. It’s illegal to overtake on Palauan roads, but drivers often overtake with poor safety awareness."]},{"k":"p","s":["In rural areas roads have limited signage and hazard warnings. Avoid driving after dark, as roads are unlit and you could hit people or livestock."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a common cause of accidents. Watch out for erratic driving, particularly at night and weekends."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rainy season and flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["Palau is vulnerable to tropical cyclones and floods. There is higher rainfall from April to November, peaking in July and August. Severe downpours can cause floods and damage roads."]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor local and international weather updates from the World Meteorological Organization and the Japanese Meteorological Agency and follow any instructions issued by the local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 911 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Palau","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/172/palau#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Palau","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/172/palau"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zika virus"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Belau National Hospital in Koror has a pharmacy that dispenses basic medicines."]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Palau"]},{"k":"p","s":["Health facilities in Palau are adequate for routine medical care. The only national hospital is Belau National Hospital in Koror, which is less than 2 hours from most locations. The hospital has a hyperbaric chamber to treat patients in diving accidents and can carry out operations."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are small number of private clinics and community health centres that offer basic primary and urgent care services. Doctors and medical centres often expect immediate cash payment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Emergency treatment or treatment for serious conditions is limited so may require medical evacuation. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Palau"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 911 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/palau/british-embassy-manila"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/palau/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":11191,"change":"New information about dual nationals returning to the UK (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/sudans-death-toll-in-2025-could-be-as-high-as-100-000-how-we-did-the-count-292159":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"80ffee7dd4705146ac6561fabb5eba937f55bc7f9a379b56754fafb8d14a2080","read":"https://theconversation.com/sudans-death-toll-in-2025-could-be-as-high-as-100-000-how-we-did-the-count-292159","bytes":191285,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Sudan’s death toll in 2025 could be as high as 100,000: how we did the count","by":[{"n":"Paola Vesco","r":"Senior Researcher, Peace Research Institute Oslo (PRIO)"},{"n":"Amber Deniz","r":"Senior Analyst, Uppsala University"},{"n":"David Randahl","r":"Associate Senior Lecturer in War Studies, Swedish Defence University"},{"n":"Johan Brosché","r":"Associate Professor, Department of Peace and Conflict Research, Uppsala University"},{"n":"Magnus Öberg","r":"Director of the Uppsala Conflict Data Program, Uppsala University"}],"pub":"2026-10-06T13:19:42Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Estimates of the number of people directly killed in ",["Sudan’s civil war","https://acleddata.com/country/sudan"]," vary widely, in part because counting casualties in active conflict zones is extremely difficult. In 2025, for instance, one conflict monitoring organisation reported around ",["19,000 direct deaths from political violence","https://acleddata.com/country/sudan"]," across Sudan. Other research teams put the toll at ",["more than 70,000","https://ucdp.uu.se/country/625"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Conflict researcher Paola Vesco and her team have ",["developed a model","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/00220027261423826"]," that helps measure the uncertainty around such conflict death estimates. Their approach takes into account the tendency of conflict datasets to underestimate fatalities, and provides a more realistic range of possible death tolls. They explain how their model could improve estimates in conflicts like Sudan’s."]},{"k":"h","s":["What do we know about the number of dead in Sudan?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The number of people killed in Sudan’s war since it began in April 2023 ",["remains contested","https://www.csis.org/analysis/sudan-war-10-charts"],". Deaths in 2025 have been especially ",["hard to establish","https://sudantribune.com/article/317893"]," as communications were cut and humanitarian access was blocked."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2025, fighting intensified as the ",["Rapid Support Forces","https://theconversation.com/how-militia-groups-capture-states-and-ruin-countries-the-case-of-sudans-rapid-support-forces-234650"]," – a paramilitary group which has fought the Sudanese army for control of the state since 2023 – ",["advanced in Darfur and North Kordofan","https://amaniafrica-et.org/briefing-on-the-situation-in-sudan-16-december-2025/"],". Civilian deaths and displacement rose."]},{"k":"p","s":["In February 2026, a UN fact-finding mission concluded that the Rapid Support Forces’ killings in El-Fasher bore the hallmarks of ",["genocide","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/02/1166997"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The group’s capture of ",["El-Fasher in the Darfur region in October 2025","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/02/1166997"],", after an 18-month siege, marked a significant territorial and strategic shift in the war. It consolidated the Rapid Support Forces’ control over much of Darfur and strengthened their position in western Sudan. It was also one of the war’s deadliest episodes."]},{"k":"p","s":["ACLED, a global conflict monitoring organisation, ",["reported around 19,000 deaths","https://acleddata.com/conflict-data/data-export-tool"],", which it describes as a ",["conservative minimum count","https://acleddata.com/methodology/how-interpret-acleds-fatality-records-sudan"]," of deaths directly caused by political violence in Sudan in 2025."]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Uppsala Conflict Data Program","https://ucdp.uu.se/country/625"],", a research team at Uppsala University that has recorded conflict deaths worldwide for decades, estimated that attacks against civilians killed at least 60,000 people. More than 10,000 died in fighting between the army and armed groups."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drawing on survivors’ reports, satellite imagery and other evidence, ",["Yale’s Humanitarian Research Lab","https://www.irishtimes.com/world/africa/2025/12/05/at-least-60000-murdered-in-sudanese-city-which-resembles-a-slaughterhouse/"]," estimates that at least 60,000 people were killed during and after the paramilitary group’s capture of El-Fasher between late October and the end of December 2025."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even worse, these figures capture only direct deaths from violence in Sudan. Many more have probably died from disease and starvation, as the Rapid Support Forces built ",["earthen walls around El-Fasher","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4gz1ekdee3o"]," in an effort to starve out the population."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why are the estimates so different?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The true number of war deaths can ",["never be known with certainty","https://theconversation.com/sudans-civil-war-has-left-at-least-62-000-dead-by-our-estimate-but-the-true-figure-could-be-far-higher-242073"],", but scholars understand reasonably well why estimates vary."]},{"k":"p","s":["Much of the difference across reports stems from how organisations select and handle sources."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Uppsala Conflict Data Program’s estimate of 60,000 killed in El-Fasher draws directly on Yale’s Humanitarian Research Lab. It is corroborated by independent reporting from Sudanese media, NGOs and ",["UN agencies","https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/documents/countries/sudan/2026-02-13-rsf-offensive-fasher-capture-1-en.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Beyond this “best” (most likely) figure, Uppsala’s programme also keeps a “high” estimate (75,000), reflecting the upper end of Yale’s assessment, and a markedly lower “low” estimate to flag that the exact number of deaths is uncertain."]},{"k":"p","s":["ACLED, on the other hand, reports a ",["single fatality number","https://acleddata.com/methodology/fatalities"],". When sources disagree, it takes the most conservative figure."]},{"k":"p","s":["The differences in the estimates of deaths also stem from how monitoring organisations process information."]},{"k":"p","s":["Both the Uppsala Conflict Data Program and ACLED record the exact date and location of violent incidents when these are available. But ACLED only counts deaths that can be ",["linked to a specific incident","https://acleddata.com/methodology/how-interpret-acleds-fatality-records-sudan"],". Deaths reported only as a broad total, without identifiable incidents, may therefore be left out. Uppsala’s programme instead records them as a “summary” event, which presents the total death toll over a longer period or a wider area, rather than for a single attack."]},{"k":"p","s":["This apparently simple rule matters enormously when many smaller, undated incidents add up to a large total, as in El-Fasher."]},{"k":"h","s":["What did you do differently and what numbers did you come up with?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Providing an uncertainty estimate – a plausible range rather than a single number of people who may have died in war – reflects how much confidence an estimate deserves, and invites caution."]},{"k":"p","s":["Still, most sources report only one number."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a recently published ",["article","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/00220027261423826"],", we built an uncertainty estimate. We surveyed Uppsala Conflict Data Program’s expert coders to build a model converting any single number into a range of people that have plausibly died in a given war. This range is known as a “probability distribution”."]},{"k":"p","s":["We applied this model to Sudan in 2025. The results are provided in the figure below. For each number of deaths on the horizontal axis, the height of the curve shows how plausible that number is according to our model. The higher the curve, the more plausible the number."]},{"k":"p","s":["The peak of the curve from our model indicates that the most likely death toll in Sudan’s war in 2025 is close to 80,000 deaths. As our model is estimated from the Uppsala programme, it is unsurprising that this estimate is close to what they report."]},{"k":"p","s":["We find that the true number of people killed in 2025 is very likely (with a 90% probability) to fall between 73,000 and 90,000, and there is a small chance that the actual number is as high as 100,000."]},{"k":"p","s":["This relatively small range indicates that the uncertainty around Sudan’s deaths is much narrower than assumed – even the lower bound number far exceeds many official estimates."]},{"k":"p","s":["Our estimates, however, should be read with caution. They are informed by the Uppsala Conflict Data Program’s knowledge and only indirectly reflect other estimates. Crucially, our model results cannot be checked against the true number of deaths, which tends to remain unknown as conflict actors have incentives to conceal it."]},{"k":"p","s":["Despite these caveats, providing a range of people that plausibly have died in war instead of a single figure gives weight to the estimate, and leaves little room to dismiss inconvenient numbers as unreliable."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why are these data points important?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Understanding how conflict death estimates vary across sources, and why, increases transparency."]},{"k":"p","s":["Around the ",["attack on El-Fasher","https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/mar/25/heroism-horror-and-the-pits-of-hell-inside-the-last-days-of-el-fasher"],", the uncertainty around death reports became a political issue. We argue that it should be a scientific one. One that can be measured."]},{"k":"p","s":["The evidence suggests that the El-Fasher massacre ",["could have been prevented","https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/jun/23/uk-ties-uae-mass-atrocities-sudan-mps-select-committee-nathaniel-raymond"],". Throughout the siege, NGOs and academic institutions repeatedly warned foreign officials of an impending bloodbath."]},{"k":"p","s":["Pressure against the ",["United Arab Emirates","https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/jun/23/uk-ties-uae-mass-atrocities-sudan-mps-select-committee-nathaniel-raymond"]," – the Rapid Support Forces’ main supporter – could have curbed the flow of weapons to the group. Yet, western politicians prioritised ",["economic and political ties with Abu Dhabi","https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/uk-failed-act-el-fasher-genocide-because-uae-mps-told"]," over preventing atrocity. Officials looked the other way, ignoring or downplaying the reports of rising deaths as uncertain."]},{"k":"p","s":["Since fighting broke out between the army and the Rapid Support Forces, Sudan has become one of the world’s ",["worst humanitarian and displacement crises","https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/power-struggle-sudan"],". More than ",["34 million people","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/04/1167301"]," – over half the country’s population – need humanitarian assistance, including food aid. An ",["estimated 14 million people","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/04/1167301"]," have been displaced."]},{"k":"p","s":["This suffering could have been foreseen and minimised. Even in the most conservative case, our model finds that more than 70,000 people died in 2025, most of them within a few months. Are 70,000 deaths enough to look the other way?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Conflict monitoring organisations such as the Uppsala Conflict Data Program have documented war deaths for decades, drawing on vetted sources and consistent coding rules. Since the uncertainty around their figures can be quantified, as our model shows, this uncertainty is no longer a reason for delay or inaction."]},{"k":"p","s":["Decision-makers often have the information they need to act early. Used in time, this information can help prevent atrocities and save lives."]}],"chars":8049},"https://theconversation.com/the-stunning-transformation-of-the-southern-democratic-party-in-one-lifetime-296566":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-18T15:33Z","sha":"e93a1c14357c3d09405995279fe88a9d4c97a3decc63e894f66cc8788cdd83e4","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-stunning-transformation-of-the-southern-democratic-party-in-one-lifetime-296566","bytes":189578,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The stunning transformation of the Southern Democratic Party, in one lifetime","by":[{"n":"Gibbs Knotts","r":"Professor of Political Science, Coastal Carolina University"},{"n":"Drew Kurlowski","r":"Associate Professor of Political Science, Coastal Carolina University"}],"pub":"2026-11-18T13:35:40Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Two of the biggest surprises of the 2026 election cycle were Democratic U.S. Senate victories in the Deep South. In addition to ",["Annie Andrews’ victory in South Carolina","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/03/us/andrews-graham-south-carolina-senate.html"],", ",["Scott Colom won a close contest in Mississippi","https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/us/elections/colom-mississippi-senate-democrats.html"],", a place that historian James Cobb called “ ",["The Most Southern Place on Earth","https://www.amazon.com/dp/0195089138?lv=shuf&channelId=500&plpRedirect=mhFallback"],".”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Colom is not Mississippi’s first Black U.S. senator. ",["Two Black Republicans represented the state during Reconstruction","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cmde1y19g19do"],", when state legislatures still selected U.S. senators. But ",["Colom is the state’s first popularly elected Black U.S. senator","https://apnews.com/article/scott-colom-mississippi-senate-midterms-56c18f292a734443992f9466c3b0f346"],". He is also ",["the first Democrat that Mississippians have sent to the Senate since 1982","https://www.reuters.com/world/us/democrat-scott-colom-defeats-incumbent-cindy-hyde-smith-cnn-projects-2026-11-11/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is tempting to read this change using a familiar political science term: ",["party realignment","https://www.acsu.buffalo.edu/%7Ejcampbel/documents/SSHRealignment06.pdf"],". But as political scientists who study ",["political campaigns","https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=6P3QreQAAAAJ&hl=en"]," and ",["Southern politics","https://www.coastal.edu/academics/facultyprofiles/educationandsocialsciences/politics/drewkurlowski/"],", we see an equally important transformation in the electorate – the people who can cast votes."]},{"k":"p","s":["Simply put, the Democratic Party in the South was transformed from a ",["party that defended white supremacy and Black disenfranchisement","https://www.thirteen.org/wnet/jimcrow/stories_org_democratic.html"]," into one in which ",["Black voters became a central constituency","https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2508.2004.00287.x"],". The Democrats who held Mississippi’s Senate seats for the majority of the 20th century were chosen by an electorate built to exclude Black citizens. Colom was elected by a dramatically different electorate and represents a very different Democratic Party."]},{"k":"p","s":["To understand the magnitude of the change in Mississippi, it helps to look at the Democrats who came before Colom, what they stood for, and the political power they wielded."]},{"k":"h","s":["Reconstruction and its end"]},{"k":"p","s":["For a brief period after the Civil War, Black Mississippians exercised real political power. They were a majority of the state’s population and, with the right to vote, helped elect the state Legislature that sent ",["Hiram Revels to the U.S. Senate in 1870 and Blanche Bruce in 1875","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cmde1y19g19do"],", both of them Black."]},{"k":"p","s":["That period did not last. White Democrats regained control of Mississippi government in the 1870s, ",["aided by political violence, intimidation and electoral fraud","https://aquila.usm.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1226&context=jmh"],". In 1890, ",["the state adopted a new constitution","https://www.mshistorynow.mdah.ms.gov/issue/mississippi-constitution-of-1890-as-originally-adopted"]," that used poll taxes, literacy tests and other restrictions to systematically disfranchise Black voters. Two years after the new constitution took effect, the percentage of Black men registered to vote had fallen to just ",["5.4 percent","https://www.newspapers.com/article/enterprise-journal-after-43-years-repub/169396290/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The result fundamentally changed Mississippi politics. For the next several generations, the Democratic Party dominated a political system in which most Black Mississippians had little meaningful voice. Republican electoral victories were rare. One ",["newspaper in 1963","https://www.newspapers.com/article/enterprise-journal-after-43-years-repub/169396290/"]," claimed that the election of Lewis McAllister to the state House was the party’s first win in 43 years. Without a competitive general election, winning the Democratic primary often meant winning office, and the politicians who emerged from a one-party system made possible by Black disenfranchisement became some of the most powerful political figures in the state’s history."]},{"k":"p","s":["What happened in Mississippi reflected a broader pattern across the South. In 1949, political scientist V.O. Key published his seminal study of the region, “ ",["Southern Politics in State and Nation","https://utpress.org/9780870494352/southern-politics-in-state-and-nation/"],".” Key argued that the solid Democratic South rested on systematic disenfranchisement of Black voters."]},{"k":"p","s":["The one-party system helped keep Black Southerners out of political power while allowing white Southern politicians to present a unified front against efforts to dismantle segregation and expand Black voting rights, particularly when those efforts came from the federal government. Mississippi was among the clearest examples of that system."]},{"k":"h","s":["Segregation and political power"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before Colom, the last Democrat elected to the ",["U.S. Senate from Mississippi was John Stennis in 1982","https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/S000852"],". He first won election in 1947, filling the seat of ",["Theodore Bilbo","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Theodore-G-Bilbo"],", who had won reelection in 1946 after a campaign in which ",["he urged white Mississippians to keep Black voters away from the polls","https://auetd.auburn.edu/bitstream/handle/10415/3040/wakefield_zach_ma_thesis_auetd_upload_resubmit_20120412.pdf?sequence=2&isAllowed=y"],". ",["Stennis won a 1947 special election with 52,068 votes","https://doi.org/10.1177/1532673X7900700307"]," in a state of 2.2 million people. He held that seat for 41 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Throughout much of his career, ",["Stennis was an ardent segregationist","https://www.navytimes.com/news/your-navy/2020/06/23/john-c-stennis-was-an-ardent-segregationist-is-it-time-to-change-the-carriers-name/"],". In 1956, he was one of 101 members of Congress from the South who signed what was called the “ ",["Southern Manifesto","https://www.crmvet.org/docs/560312_southern_manifesto.pdf"],".” In that statement, these lawmakers denounced the Supreme Court’s decision in ",["Brown v. Board of Education","https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/347us483"],", in which the justices ruled that the “separate but equal” standards of racial segregation were unconstitutional; Stennis and the other signatories pledged resistance to school desegregation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Later in his Senate career, Stennis was ",["the only Southern Democrat who voted to oppose","https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/opinions/articles/solemn_senate_votes_for_national_holiday.html"]," the creation of a national holiday honoring Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr."]},{"k":"p","s":["Stennis’ Senate colleague for much of his tenure, Mississippi ",["Democrat James Eastland","https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/E000018?__cf_chl_tk=nSsQRKOSHVDkKItjRP1VujR16jCfSkjLRJeh0LNd3_k-1794860208-1.0.1.1-uEE9VKLsL.F29i99Rlu6ftRueSq6ObDeFuYZF.ltxJs"],", was an equally staunch defender of segregation. A cotton planter from the Mississippi Delta, he was ",["appointed to the Senate in 1941 and won the seat outright in 1942","https://www.senate.gov/senators/FeaturedBios/Featured_Bio_EastlandJames.htm"],". He remained in the Senate until 1978."]},{"k":"p","s":["The day after the Supreme Court handed down its 1954 decision in Brown v. Board of Education, ",["Eastland was quoted saying","https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/post-nation/wp/2014/05/16/how-the-washington-post-covered-brown-v-board-of-education-in-1954/"],", “the South will not abide by nor obey this legislative decision by a political court.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["Recent attention has understandably focused on Stennis, the last Democrat to represent Mississippi in the Senate. When Colom takes office, he will occupy the Senate seat once held by Eastland and ",["Democrat James Vardaman","https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/v000070"],", who ",["said that","https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/flood-vardaman/"]," “if it is necessary every Negro in the state will be lynched; it will be done to maintain white supremacy.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["One-party rule also gave Mississippi’s senators unusual power. With little electoral competition, they could remain in office for decades. In a Senate where seniority mattered, longevity translated into influence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Eastland, for example, chaired the Senate Judiciary Committee for more than two decades, giving him enormous influence over civil rights legislation. During his chairmanship of the powerful committee, ",["that committee became known as","https://www.senate.gov/legislative/landmark-legislation/civil-rights.htm"]," “the graveyard of civil rights legislation,” according to the Senate’s website."]},{"k":"h","s":["A new Southern coalition"]},{"k":"p","s":[["The Voting Rights Act of 1965","https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R47520"]," helped dismantle the electoral system that had sustained these men in office. Before it, ",["fewer than 10% of Black Mississippians were even registered to vote","https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199873821.001.0001"],". Within a few short years, a majority were."]},{"k":"p","s":["Black voters increasingly aligned with the Democratic Party as its national leaders embraced civil rights, even while many Democratic officeholders in Mississippi continued to defend segregation. That meant ",["Black voters sometimes supported incumbent white segregationists","https://www.npr.org/2016/08/25/491389942/when-african-american-voters-shifted-away-from-the-gop"],". The party’s voters changed faster than many of its candidates did."]},{"k":"p","s":["The 1978 election for the seat Colom will hold illustrates how complicated that transition could be. After Eastland retired, Charles Evers, brother of slain civil rights leader Medgar Evers, ",["ran as an independent and drew nearly a quarter of the vote","https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1978/11/08/cochran-winning-senate-seat-in-miss/6f7c49e3-170f-44a4-a4db-c56ac05bb68e/"],". His candidacy split the traditionally Democratic vote, helping Republican Thad Cochran win with a mere plurality."]},{"k":"p","s":["By the 21st century, ",["white Mississippians overwhelmingly supported Republican","https://www.nytimes.com/2010/11/07/us/07south.html"]," candidates, and ",["Republican dominance had replaced the old Democratic dominance","https://www.hup.harvard.edu/books/9780674012486"]," in most statewide elections."]},{"k":"p","s":["That pattern made ",["Colom’s victory all the more striking","https://www.ms.now/opinion/why-scott-colom-mississippi-senate-win-matters-2026"],". He won with overwhelming support from Black voters while also attracting enough white voters to build a winning statewide coalition."]},{"k":"p","s":["The contrast with Mississippi’s old Democratic coalition could hardly be sharper. The Democratic Party of Vardaman, Bilbo, Eastland and Stennis maintained its power in part by keeping Black Mississippians away from the ballot box with violence and Jim Crow laws."]},{"k":"p","s":["When Colom is sworn in, Revels’ old seat will, for the first time, be held by a Black senator directly elected by Mississippi voters."]}],"chars":7742},"https://theconversation.com/the-curious-history-of-the-huntaway-new-zealands-unusually-loud-sheepdog-296876":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-23T18:37Z","sha":"0ea70d8d5a033ba5b0857d5bd064de6dff4c8d5aae846ce69ed116e657e5889d","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-curious-history-of-the-huntaway-new-zealands-unusually-loud-sheepdog-296876","bytes":178892,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The curious history of the huntaway, New Zealand’s unusually loud sheepdog","by":[{"n":"Gareth Enticott","r":"Professor of Human Geography, Cardiff University; University of Waikato"}],"pub":"2026-11-23T17:29:43Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Sheepdog trials don’t always take place in a field. Some take place in a pub."]},{"k":"p","s":["Across New Zealand, ",["“bark-ups”","https://www.instagram.com/p/DKREaLkSO_g/"]," are a common sight at pubs and agricultural events, and feature a home-bred invention: the New Zealand huntaway dog breed. The aim of the bark-up is simple: find the dog with the best bark."]},{"k":"p","s":["To outsiders, the spectacle raises an obvious question: why would anyone hold a competition to find the sheepdog that barks the loudest? The answer lies not just in the sheep stations of New Zealand where the huntaway was developed, but in the colonisation of New Zealand, for sheepdogs are breeds of empire. It’s something I explore in my ",["new book","https://www.routledge.com/Making-Sense-of-Sheepdogs-Breeds-of-Empire-and-the-Sound-of-the-Global-Countryside/Enticott/p/book/9781032872759"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The modern sheepdog was a Victorian invention. Queen Victoria herself was a ",["fan of the collie","https://www.rct.uk/collection/2908315/queen-victoria-and-sharp"],", buying the best from shepherds on the England-Scotland borders. Those shepherds were at the helm of the emerging sheepdog trialling scene at the start of the 20th century."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the process, sheepdogs came to embody Victorian moral values. The ideal dog displayed discipline, intelligence, restraint, obedience and industriousness, and perhaps most of all, silence while working – qualities that were equally applicable to the shepherd."]},{"k":"p","s":["Organisations such as the International Sheep Dog Society reinforced these ideals. Under its influential honorary secretary, James Reid, the border collie was invented not merely as a useful farm dog but as the pinnacle of canine improvement."]},{"k":"p","s":["Exported throughout Britain’s settler colonies, it became a breed of empire, carrying with it particular ideas about good stockmanship, moral character and agricultural modernity. Reid went so far as to describe the sheepdog as the foundation of the pastoral economy, whose intelligence and labour underpinned the success of the British empire."]},{"k":"p","s":["Scottish settlers had already taken their sheepdogs to New Zealand in the mid-19th Century. But it would be a Northumbrian, James Lilico, who would transform New Zealand’s sheepdogs. Arriving in 1894 to work as a shepherd, he immediately sent home for his dog Captain, disappointed by the “useless mongrels” he had been given to work."]},{"k":"p","s":["So started half a century of importing the finest sheepdogs he could source, usually championship winning trial dogs, earning him the title of the “collie king of New Zealand”. Lilico imported sheepdogs that had been bred with the quality known as “eye” (herding using an intense stare) that had become highly valued among triallists. Breeding them, Lilico championed the superiority of what he called “border dogs” in his weekly column in the New Zealand Farmer periodical."]},{"k":"p","s":["Lilico’s actions were not universally welcomed, in particular his criticism of the sheepdogs and shepherds who worked New Zealand’s high country. For the shepherds of these regions, Lilico’s strong-eyed dogs were too slow, too delicate and insufficiently forceful. In their view, sheep needed to be moved, not mesmerised."]},{"k":"p","s":["Their alternative was the huntaway. What marked out the huntaway from the border collie was its noise. Without fences and across wide open country, noise had become a valuable tool for shepherds. Before setting out to muster sheep, shepherds gathered their dogs and instructed them to bark. This bark up – just like those now seen in pubs – guided sheep from a distance, so by the time shepherds arrived the sheep were already on the move."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Your dog may be wilder than you think, according to canine sleep research","https://theconversation.com/your-dog-may-be-wilder-than-you-think-according-to-canine-sleep-research-241981"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If its bark united the huntaway, their appearance did anything but. Old photographs reveal a variety of different appearances and types: from small and fluffy to larger looking collies. But as sheep farming expanded from the South Island into the hill country of the North Island, the huntaway began to evolve."]},{"k":"p","s":["To make the huntaway more powerful to deal with stubborn ",["Romney sheep","https://www.rbst.org.uk/watchlist-breed/romney/"],", its voice got louder. Breeding experiments involving mastiff, rottweiler, labrador, hound and other breeds sought to improve its barking power. A bigger huntaway became more common, but even now the huntaway defies standardisation beyond its capacity to bark all day."]},{"k":"p","s":["Crucially, while the development of New Zealand agriculture was part of a scientific ",["“grasslands revolution”","https://teara.govt.nz/en/farming-and-the-environment/page-3"],", these scientists had little to do with sheepdogs. The huntaway instead reflected the spirit of farmers, as a “practical dog for practical men”. Scientists and breed societies were kept well away in fear of ruining the dog."]},{"k":"h","s":["Recognition and uncertainty"]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2013 the huntaway was ",["recognised","https://www.dogsnz.org.nz/breeds/info/nz-huntaway/535"]," as a unique breed by the New Zealand Kennel Club, but its origins were still uncertain. Recent ",["genetic studies","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12445248/"]," show that it is related to the border collie, and other breeds imported in the 19th century. Genetic traces of the breeds used for power are harder to find, reinforcing the mythology that surrounds the breed."]},{"k":"p","s":["It may also be that its defining feature – the bark – is not simply a New Zealand invention but an echo of its past. Back in Scotland in 1935, James Reid had given a lecture celebrating the border collie conquering all other breeds of sheepdog. Its popularity had meant that other barking sheepdogs used across Wales and southern England had no place, consigning them to history."]},{"k":"p","s":["When James Lilico wrote about the huntaway in one of his three books, he received a letter from a shepherd in north Wales suggesting that the Huntaway sounded no different to the dog known as ci hanos, a dog that used noise to drive sheep onto the hills in the springtime."]},{"k":"p","s":["Lilico had planned on importing one of these as part of a breeding experiment. Such an experiment might be more valuable today as the huntaway gene pool narrows: the closure of large sheep stations in New Zealand is making breeding selection a challenge. In doing so, it raises existential questions about sheepdogs themselves, because without sheep there will be no sheepdogs."]}],"chars":5801},"https://theconversation.com/defence-literacy-must-become-a-national-security-priority-in-canada-297120":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-24T20:25Z","sha":"5b7d8769a81831620f86c6590199ba172f57ccbc695b06adc32edbb96ff0d04d","read":"https://theconversation.com/defence-literacy-must-become-a-national-security-priority-in-canada-297120","bytes":182035,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Defence literacy must become a national security priority in Canada","by":[{"n":"Kevin Budning","r":"Director, Scientific Research at the CDA Institute and Research Fellow, Norman Paterson School of International Affairs, Carleton University"}],"pub":"2026-11-24T17:08:16Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Over the past 18 months, the Canadian government has ",["committed record levels of funding to national defence","https://www.budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/chap5-en.html?"],". It has advanced major military procurement projects, expanded international defence partnerships and introduced new institutions, strategies and policies. These initiatives are aimed at rebuilding the Canadian Armed Forces and strengthening Canada’s defence industrial base."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet a critical element of this effort remains largely absent: improving public understanding of the threats facing Canada and the rationale behind these investments."]},{"k":"p","s":["Without a more defence-literate population, sustaining this historical level of spending may prove just as challenging as securing the political commitments that made them possible."]},{"k":"h","s":["What is defence literacy?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Defence literacy refers to the public’s understanding of two critical dimensions of national security:"]},{"k":"li","s":["The threats facing Canada, ranging from conventional military activity, such as long-range missile attacks and an increased ",["Russian and Chinese military presence in the Arctic","https://international.canada.ca/en/global-affairs/corporate/reports/arctic-policy-2024"]," and North Atlantic, to unconventional or grey-zone threats that extend beyond the battlefield."]},{"k":"li","s":["The policies, institutions and actions through which the government seeks to address these threats."]},{"k":"p","s":["For decades, national defence received little attention in Canadian public debate, owing in part to a relatively stable world order and American security guarantees."]},{"k":"p","s":["This false sense of security, however, has left Canadians with a significant blind spot. According to a 2021 ",["Department of National Defence survey","https://www.publicsafety.gc.ca/cnt/ntnl-scrt/cntr-trrrsm/pblc-pnn-rsrch-snpsht-2021-en.aspx"],", four in 10 Canadians could not identify a specific threat to Canada’s security or sovereignty. Similarly, a ",["2026 survey","https://publications.gc.ca/site/archivee-archived.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fpublications.gc.ca%2Fcollections%2Fcollection_2026%2Fscrs-csis%2FPS74-8-2026-eng.pdf"]," commissioned by the Canadian Security Intelligence Service found that 35 per cent of Canadians could not name an agency responsible for investigating national security threats."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why it matters"]},{"k":"p","s":["This deficit in defence literacy is more than just a knowledge gap. It reflects a longstanding failure to take national security seriously or fully understand its implications. Addressing this shortcoming, then, is important for at least four reasons:"]},{"k":"p","s":["1. Failing to do so undermines democratic accountability. Defence literacy should not be confused with defence advocacy: its purpose is not to persuade Canadians to support specific policies or military expenditures. Rather, it equips them with the knowledge necessary to understand, scrutinize and critically evaluate the decisions being made on defence, and hold their elected officials to account for those decisions—including at the ballot box."]},{"k":"p","s":["2. Greater defence literacy strengthens national resilience. Grey-zone activities including foreign interference, cyberattacks and disinformation target individuals, communities and institutions beyond the traditional military sphere. ",["The public is the target","https://www.canada.ca/en/security-intelligence-service/corporate/publications/csis-public-report-2025/operations-and-analysis.html"],", and often the first line of defence. Canada’s ability to counter these threats depends heavily on whether Canadians can recognize, respond to, and mitigate their impact. A public that understands the threats facing the country is far better equipped to do so."]},{"k":"p","s":["3. An informed public anchors defence investment beyond episodic responses to emerging crises. Improving Canada’s military capabilities requires sustained political commitment over decades, which is far more durable if rooted in public understanding. Crisis-driven support fades once the threat passes, whereas understanding the rationale behind defence priorities outlasts changes in government."]},{"k":"p","s":["4. Canada’s expanding defence ambitions require thousands of new soldiers, educators, researchers, entrepreneurs and public servants, all of whom have important roles to play in strengthening national security. Introducing the broader Canadian public to these opportunities is essential to cultivating the next generation of leaders and professionals."]},{"k":"h","s":["Building a more defence-literate future"]},{"k":"p","s":["Strengthening defence literacy cannot fall to government alone. It requires a whole-of-society approach bringing together academia, civil society and industry, all of which are instrumental in fostering a more informed and engaged population."]},{"k":"p","s":["Government must first communicate more openly with Canadians about the threats facing the country and the measures being taken to address them. Canada’s new ",["National Security Strategy","https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/pco-bcp/images/pco2/pm/nss/The_Plan_for_a_Stronger_Canada_2026.pdf"]," is a start in recognizing this need, but more must be done."]},{"k":"p","s":["Canada should learn from its Nordic allies, who treat public awareness and preparedness as central to national security. Sweden, for example, has distributed ",["guidance to households","https://www.mcf.se/en/advice-for-individuals/the-brochure-in-case-of-crisis-or-war/download-and-order-the-brochure-in-case-of-crisis-or-war/"]," explaining how to prepare for crises and war, while Finland has adopted a ",["comprehensive security model","https://turvallisuuskomitea.fi/en/comprehensive-security/"]," emphasizing co-operation between government, businesses, civil society and citizens."]},{"k":"p","s":["Think tanks, universities and civil society organizations are also integral also in making defence and security issues accessible to the public. Through research, public conferences, media, educational initiatives and opportunities for dialogue, these institutions translate complex policy issues into informed public debate."]},{"k":"p","s":["In my work at the ",["CDA Institute","https://cdainstitute.ca/"],", a Canadian defence and security think tank, I see the value of these efforts firsthand, and of a further one: investing in young Canadians. Through student conferences, mentorship programmes, internships and student-led initiatives, we help cultivate the next generation of defence and security leaders."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["How the Canadian Armed Forces could help solve the youth employment crisis","https://theconversation.com/how-the-canadian-armed-forces-could-help-solve-the-youth-employment-crisis-268433"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Industry, too, has a role in helping Canadians understand how investments in national defence are inextricably tied to more job creation, technological innovation and economic prosperity."]},{"k":"p","s":["As Canada undertakes its ",["biggest increase in defence spending since the Second World War","https://budget.canada.ca/2026/report-rapport/chap5-en.html"],", it cannot afford to neglect the importance of an informed public. These investments only stand a chance of being sustained over time if accompanied by an equally strong commitment to helping Canadians understand why they are so important."]},{"k":"p","s":["Canada’s long-term security depends on both."]}],"chars":6011},"https://theconversation.com/victorian-labor-in-danger-of-losing-opposition-status-at-election-with-former-premiers-seats-likely-to-be-lost-297280":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-25T04:04Z","sha":"45bdc3c24380012ebe15913ba16342f19228f6f6512e728d7651fbc14035b0bd","read":"https://theconversation.com/victorian-labor-in-danger-of-losing-opposition-status-at-election-with-former-premiers-seats-likely-to-be-lost-297280","bytes":181954,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Victorian Labor in danger of losing opposition status at election, with former premiers’ seats likely to be lost","by":[{"n":"Adrian Beaumont","r":"Election Analyst (Psephologist) at The Conversation; and Honorary Associate, School of Mathematics and Statistics, The University of Melbourne"}],"pub":"2026-11-25T03:19:03Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The Victorian election is on Saturday with polls closing at 6pm AEDT. There are a total of 88 lower house seats, elected in single-member electorates by preferential voting. All 40 upper house seats are also up for election in eight five-member electorates by proportional representation with preferences."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the ",["2022 Victorian election","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Victorian_state_election"],", Labor won 56 of the 88 lower house seats, the Coalition 28 and the Greens four, from a two-party vote of 55.0–45.0 to Labor over the Coalition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Of the five most recent Victorian polls (four in this article and the Redbridge poll from ",["Monday’s article","https://theconversation.com/albaneses-ratings-plunge-to-new-low-in-newspoll-victorian-coalition-set-to-win-election-297157"],"), the best for Labor still has them trailing the Coalition after preferences by 55–45, a ten-point swing to the Coalition since the 2022 election. The other four polls have Labor trailing by 58–42 or 59–41."]},{"k":"p","s":["The poll graph below includes the YouGov poll. Since mid-October, Labor’s psoition has declined in nearly every poll when compared against the last edition of that poll, with only the Morgan SMS poll showing any improvement for Labor. It appears that the more voters focus on the election, the worse it gets for Labor."]},{"k":"p","s":["The question now is not whether Labor loses the election, but whether they will retain official opposition status. Either the Greens or One Nation could win more seats than Labor to become the opposition. In a landslide for the Coalition, most of the seats a left-wing party would still win are inner-city seats where the Greens do well, so the Greens could win more seats than Labor."]},{"k":"p","s":["The YouGov MRP poll has seat projections that show Labor getting thumped by the Coalition in two former premiers’ seats: Dan Andrews’ Mulgrave and Jacinta Allan’s Bendigo East."]},{"k":"h","s":["Victorian Resolve poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["The final ",["Victorian Resolve poll","https://www.theage.com.au/politics/victoria/four-out-of-five-voters-want-anyone-but-labor-a-once-in-a-century-drubbing-looms-20261125-p61gug.html"]," for The Age, conducted November 20–23 from a sample of 1,000, gave the Coalition 31% of the primary vote (up one since the early November Resolve poll), One Nation 25% (up two), Labor 20% (down two), the Greens 13% (steady), independents 4% (down two) and others 7% (steady)."]},{"k":"p","s":["By respondent preferences, the Coalition led Labor by 59–41, a four-point gain for the Coalition. One Nation preferences favoured the Coalition by 82–18 over Labor, while Labor preferences favoured the Coalition by 80–20 over One Nation."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a three-way preferred premier question, Liberal leader Jess Wilson had 34% (down one), Labor incumbent Ben Carroll 23% (up two) and One Nation leader Warren Pickering 19% (up four)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Asked to rate the leaders’ performances during the election campaign, Wilson was at +19 and Carroll at -34."]},{"k":"h","s":["YouGov MRP poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["A Victorian ",["YouGov poll","https://www.news24.com.au/politics/australian-politics/victorian-labor-headed-for-nightmare-state-election-recording-worst-vote-since-1902-as-coalition-set-for-landslide-victory/news-story/d5f3b4209cbfc3c3c03d8afc852d8c66"]," for News24 and The Herald Sun, conducted November 6–23 from a large sample of 3,890, gave the Coalition 31% of the primary vote, One Nation 21%, Labor 20%, the Greens 16%, the Victorian Socialists 4%, independents 4% and others 5%."]},{"k":"p","s":["By respondent preferences, the Coalition led Labor by 58–42. Analyst Kevin Bonham ",["said he would have expected","https://x.com/kevinbonham/status/2124821134361415893"]," a 54–46 Coalition lead from applying past election flows to the primary votes. One Nation’s flow to the Coalition is probably very strong."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a four-party preferred, the Coalition had 33%, One Nation 24%, Labor 22% and the Greens 22%. If this is accurate, Labor would be excluded in a large number of seats, with their preferences helping the Greens."]},{"k":"p","s":["By 59–30, respondents were dissatisfied with Carroll (net -29). By 50–38, they were satisfied with Wilson (net +12). In a four-way preferred premier question, Wilson had 34%, Carroll 19%, Pickering 19% and Greens leader Ellen Sandell 17%. By 54–30, respondents ",["did not think the Suburban Rail Loop","https://www.heraldsun.com.au/news/victoria/state-election/major-poll-reveals-coalition-leads-labor-on-crime-and-victorias-economy/news-story/73fc4e1f3872c745ce4fdb931163c26e"]," was a priority."]},{"k":"p","s":["This poll was also an MRP poll, in which modelling and large sample sizes are used to estimate seat outcomes. On these results, Labor would ",["lose the seats of former premiers","https://www.news24.com.au/politics/australian-politics/labor-set-for-double-dose-of-electoral-humiliation-as-dan-andrews-and-jacinta-allans-former-seats-to-fall-to-coalition/news-story/bf6b224c5f51c617bb5840295e6fe7d9"]," Jacinta Allan (Bendigo East) and Dan Andrews (Mulgrave)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Allan had held ",["Bendigo East","https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/vic/2026/guide/beea"]," since Labor gained it in 1999 and won government. In 2022, she defeated the Liberals by 60.8–39.2, but the Nationals are beating Labor by 60–40 in this poll."]},{"k":"p","s":["Labor had held ",["Mulgrave","https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/vic/2026/guide/mulg"]," since its creation in 2002, with Andrews defeating a right-wing independent in 2022 by 60.8–39.2. In this poll, the Liberals are leading Labor by 56–44."]},{"k":"h","s":["Morgan SMS poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["A Victorian ",["Morgan SMS poll","https://www.roymorgan.com/findings/10393-victorian-state-voting-intention-november-2026"],", conducted November 18–19 from a sample of 1,563, gave the Coalition 31% of the primary vote (up 1.5 since the late October Morgan poll), Labor 22.5% (steady), One Nation 20% (down two), the Greens 13% (up 0.5), independents 5% (down 2.5) and others 8.5% (up 2.5)."]},{"k":"p","s":["By respondent preferences, the Coalition led Labor by 55–45, a one-point gain for Labor. Labor led One Nation by 54–46, a 0.5-point gain for One Nation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Synesis poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["Poll Bludger reported","https://www.pollbludger.net/2026/11/22/redbridge-group-labor-22-coalition-33-one-nation-23-in-victoria/"]," last Sunday that a Victorian Synesis poll, conducted November 10–18 from a sample of 1,772, gave the Coalition 33% of the primary vote, Labor 25%, One Nation 24%, the Greens 11% and all Others 7%. The Coalition led Labor by 59–41 after preferences. This pollster “seems to have predominantly conservative clients”, according to The Poll Bludger."]},{"k":"h","s":["How to vote card recommendations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Most voters don’t follow how to vote card recommendations, but the follow rate is higher for Labor and Coalition voters."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Poll Bludger said on November 12 that the ",["Liberals will recommend their voters","https://www.pollbludger.net/2026/11/12/redbridge-group-coalition-31-labor-23-one-nation-22-in-victoria/"]," preference Labor ahead of the Greens in most of the inner city seats, reversing their 2022 election policy when they had the Greens recommended ahead of Labor."]},{"k":"p","s":["Possibly as a trade-off, Labor won’t recommend preferences in Prahran between the Greens and the Liberals. The Liberals gained ",["Prahran","https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/vic/2026/guide/prah"]," from the Greens at a 2025 byelection by a 51.3–48.7 margin. In 2022, the Greens had defeated the Liberals by 62.0–38.0."]}],"chars":5652}};
